


During architectural evaluation, we investigated the impact of increasing the decoding MLP capacity from the baseline (e.g., 64 neurons) to a larger configuration (128 neurons, 3 hidden layers). Counter-intuitively, the larger network exhibited a degradation in spectral fidelity. As shown in Figure X, the baseline network correctly synthesizes the yellow/orange emissive core (requiring both Red and Green channel predictions), whereas the over-parameterized network regresses to a monochromatic red output, effectively dropping the Green radiance signal.We attribute this to gradient domination under the L2 loss formulation. In an emissive explosion, the physical magnitude of red radiance ($L_{red}$) exponentially dwarfs the green radiance ($L_{green}$). Because Mean Squared Error disproportionately penalizes large absolute errors, the network's optimization gradients are heavily biased toward minimizing the red channel loss.A smaller, constrained MLP acts as an implicit regularizer, forcing the network to learn correlated, shared features for the 'fire' state that preserve the secondary green emissions. Conversely, the high-capacity network possesses enough parameters to decouple the color channels. Driven by the imbalanced loss, the optimizer dedicates the expanded capacity entirely to over-fitting the high-magnitude red peaks. Consequently, the comparatively minuscule green gradients are suppressed, likely pushing the green channel weights into the zero-gradient regime of the ReLU activation function. This finding validates the Instant-NGP paradigm: high-frequency spatial details should be encoded in the Hash Grid, while the decoding MLP must remain shallow and constrained to prevent spectral overfitting.
